{#
  DAH-2544 — the system QPL (Quote Provider Library) configuration.

  THE WHOLE FILE, NOT TWO EDITED LINES.

  This was two `lineinfile` tasks, and they corrupted Intel's shipped config
  into something the QPL could not parse. Intel writes the file in
  LEADING-COMMA style — the first key is bare, every later key is prefixed
  with a comma:

      {
        "pccs_url": "https://localhost:8081/sgx/certification/v4/"
        ,"use_secure_cert": true
        ,"retry_times": 6
      }

  Against that file:

  - `regexp: '^\s*"use_secure_cert"\s*:'` never matched `,"use_secure_cert"`,
    because of the leading comma. lineinfile's documented behaviour when a
    regexp matches nothing is to APPEND, so the line landed after the closing
    brace and the file stopped being JSON.
  - `"pccs_url"` did match, and was replaced with a version carrying a TRAILING
    comma — which, followed by the next key's LEADING comma, produced a double
    comma.

  Either one alone is fatal, and the failure is silent on the host: it surfaces
  three layers away as a CVM that reboots at about 160 seconds. The QPL cannot
  read its config, quote generation returns a DCAP error, the sealing-key
  provider refuses the guest, and dstack-prepare cannot unseal the data disk —
  while the converge reports 0 MUST_FIX. Observed on au11.

  Writing the file removes the class of defect rather than the instance: no
  regexp to fail to match, no house style to guess.

  THE TWO VALUES THAT MATTER

  use_secure_cert — a local PCCS serves a SELF-SIGNED certificate. Left at
  Intel's shipped `true`, the QCNL fails with
  `CURL error: (60) SSL peer certificate ... was not OK`, then
  `[QPL] Failed to get quote config. Error code is 0xb033`.

  local_cache_only — false, so the QPL may fetch from the PCCS. true would
  require an administrator to pre-populate the cache by hand on every platform.

  THREE CONSTRAINTS ON THIS TEMPLATE ITSELF

  No `ansible_managed` banner. The output must be strict JSON, JSON has no
  comment syntax, and the task that writes this validates with `json.load`, so
  a banner fails the converge. Intel's own copy uses `//` comments, which their
  parser tolerates and `json.load` does not.

  No Jinja comments below this block. Ansible renders templates with
  trim_blocks on, which eats the newline after a closing comment tag, so an
  inline comment silently joins the next key onto the previous line.

  Nothing in this block may contain a closing comment tag, even inside prose or
  a code sample: it ends the comment early and every remaining line is written
  into the file. That is why the sequence is described in words above rather
  than shown.
#}
{
  "pccs_url": "{{ lium_pccs_url }}",
  "use_secure_cert": {{ lium_qcnl_use_secure_cert | bool | lower }},
  "retry_times": {{ lium_qcnl_retry_times }},
  "retry_delay": {{ lium_qcnl_retry_delay }},
  "pck_cache_expire_hours": {{ lium_qcnl_pck_cache_expire_hours }},
  "verify_collateral_cache_expire_hours": {{ lium_qcnl_collateral_cache_expire_hours }},
  "local_cache_only": false
}
